Subject: [PATCH] map shows work state, oldest first; real touch targets on
 phone and tablet
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The map
- Marker colour was the geocoding precision and nothing else, so a station
  with eleven overdue Prüfungen looked identical to one with none as long as
  both matched exactly. Colour now carries the work state, using the same
  tones as every badge and chart (shared/status-appearance.ts): amber offen,
  blue in Bearbeitung, red abgelehnt/gestoppt, green all decided.
- Precision was not discarded, it moved to the border. A region-centroid
  marker is not standing where it claims, so it is dashed and slightly
  translucent instead of reading as a confident pin.
- Stations with open or in-progress work pulse. Overdue or blocked stations
  pulse faster and wider, which is the difference you notice in peripheral
  vision — the point of a map. The ring is a separate ::after element
  animating transform and opacity only: animating width or box-shadow across
  ~900 markers forces layout every frame.
- Groups are sorted by their oldest open Prüfdatum and added to the layer in
  that order, so the reveal sweeps the map chronologically instead of
  following whatever order the rows arrived in. The stagger is budgeted at
  ~700ms total rather than per marker, and pending timers are cleared on
  cleanup so filtering mid-reveal cannot drop markers into a cleared layer.
- prefers-reduced-motion drops both the pulse and the stagger: it is
  decoration, and the count and legend already state it in text.
- The legend described the old meaning; it now describes the new one and says
  what pulsing means.

Measured over the production bundle: 425 markers, 228 pulsing, 155 of those
urgent, 8 dashed region fallbacks, and db-marker-pulse confirmed running.

Touch
- Five device profiles (iPhone SE, iPhone 14 Pro, iPad mini, iPad Pro
  portrait and landscape) across four routes: 0 horizontal overflow, 0 text
  under 11px, 0 tap targets under 44px.
- Getting there needed two rules the first pass missed. Text inputs were 36px
  tall — a field is a tap target before it is a field. And the Ja/Nein answers
  in the wizard are <label> elements wrapping a visually hidden radio, so none
  of the existing coarse-pointer rules reached them; they measured 28px, on
  the control a user touches 22 times per Anmeldung.

One measurement of mine was wrong before it was right: the first mobile probe
reported 9,093 elements wider than the viewport on /projects. It was checking
each element's own overflow-x rather than walking its ancestors, so it counted
every cell of a table sitting correctly inside a scroller. Corrected, it is 0.

Gates: 0 type errors, 129 passed / 6 skipped, 80 lint warnings, build clean,
e2e 22 passed / 0 failed.

